class Solution {

    public boolean hasPathSum(TreeNode root, int targetSum) {

        if (root == null) {
            return false;
        }

        // Check if current node is a leaf
        if (root.left == null && root.right == null) {
            return targetSum == root.val;
        }

        // Subtract current value from target
        int remainingSum = targetSum - root.val;

        return hasPathSum(root.left, remainingSum)
                || hasPathSum(root.right, remainingSum);
    }
}

//I use DFS recursion. At each node, I subtract its value from the target sum, which gives me the remaining sum needed from the rest of the path. 
//When I reach a leaf node, I check whether the remaining sum equals the leaf's value.
//I recursively check both the left and right subtrees, and return true if either subtree contains a valid root-to-leaf path."

//"The time complexity is O(n) because each node can be visited once. The space complexity is O(h) due to the recursion stack, where h is the height of the tree."